    "result_text": "The full suite ran \u2014 there's one test class (`AcceptanceTest`; the other two files are support/base classes) and it passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the GOLD membership tier rule. Following the codebase's existing pattern for creation-time derived values (like `namesakeCount` and `bulkSignupWarning`), the household size is computed when the owner is created and stored, then read by the mapper that derives the read-only tier.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `householdMemberCount` field (`household_member_count` column) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **`service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 in `saveOwner`, for new owners, store the household size *after* this create as `householdMembers.size() + 1` (existing members plus the new owner).\n\n3. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `toMembershipTier` now returns `GOLD` when `householdMemberCount >= 3`, otherwise falls through to the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules.\n\n4. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `household_member_count` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe JDBC repository uses `BeanPropertySqlParameterSource`/`BeanPropertyRowMapper`, so the new property maps automatically without changes \u2014 consistent with how `namesake_count` is handled there.",
